## Early Signs of Nail Fungus: What to Look For

The first sign is almost always easy to dismiss. A white or yellow spot at the nail tip, a slight roughness along the free edge, a texture that files away a little differently than usual. Most people assume they knocked the nail or that a gel application has left a mark. They file it back, apply another coat, and move on. Months later, the spot has become a stripe, and the nail is noticeably thicker.

The primary visible signs of <strong>nail fungus</strong> are: discolouration (white, yellow, brown, or occasionally dark), thickening of the nail plate, brittleness or crumbling particularly at the nail edges, a chalky or powdery texture on the nail surface, and in more advanced cases, nail separation from the nail bed. These signs do not all appear at once.

What tends to happen is a predictable progression. Early <strong>nail fungus</strong> presents as a small area of discolouration  most commonly at the tip or side of the nail, most commonly on a toe rather than a finger. Over weeks to months, that discolouration spreads. The nail begins to thicken, accumulating material underneath that lifts it slightly off the nail bed. The free edge starts to crumble. The nail may take on an uneven, ridged surface. In some cases  particularly with endonyx type  the surface looks relatively normal while the inside of the nail plate is already compromised.

One thing worth naming directly: the smell. A nail infected with fungus can develop a faint but distinct odour from the debris that accumulates beneath it. This is one of the more reliable distinguishing signs, and one that competitors rarely mention.

Darker skin tones present slightly differently  the discolouration may appear as a brown or grey change rather than the classic yellow, which can cause the infection to be overlooked or misattributed at an earlier stage.

