## What Causes Nail Fungus and How It Enters the Nail

Fungi are everywhere. That is the starting point  not a warning, just a fact. The organisms that cause <strong>nail fungus</strong> exist in the environment around us constantly, and exposure is unavoidable. What separates those who develop an infection from those who don't is not exposure but vulnerability: a break in the seal, a change in immune response, or an environment that tips the balance in the fungus's favour.

A dermatophyte is a type of fungus that feeds on keratin, the protein that makes nails hard  and it is responsible for the majority of nail fungus infections worldwide. The two other organism groups are yeasts (predominantly *Candida albicans*, more common in fingernail infections) and non-dermatophyte moulds such as *Fusarium* and *Aspergillus*, which account for a smaller but meaningful proportion of cases  particularly in tropical climates or in people with pre-existing nail damage.

The entry route matters. <strong>Nail fungus</strong> enters through any breach in the protective barrier around the nail  microtrauma from ill-fitting shoes, a small cut around the cuticle, a nail that is beginning to separate from the nail bed for any reason, or a nick from nail clippers. Warm, moist environments are the perfect incubator: the inside of shoes, communal showers, and swimming pools all provide conditions in which fungi thrive and spread.

Athlete's foot  tinea pedis  deserves particular mention. It is the skin fungal infection most often linked to subsequent nail infection, because the fungus on the surrounding skin has direct access to the nail via the skin-to-nail margin. Treating <strong>nail fungus</strong> without addressing a concurrent athlete's foot infection is one of the primary reasons infections return.

For credible clinical background on this, the <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/diseases/a-z/nail-fungus-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American Academy of Dermatology</a> provides an authoritative overview of how <strong>nail fungus</strong> spreads and is transmitted.


