## Who Is Most at Risk of Developing Nail Fungus

<strong>Nail fungus</strong> is not a hygiene failure. Framing it that way is both inaccurate and unhelpful  the people most susceptible to nail fungus are susceptible because of specific biological and lifestyle factors, not because they are somehow less careful than people who never get it.

The primary risk groups for <strong>nail fungus</strong> are: older adults (the risk increases significantly after 60 due to slower nail growth, reduced circulation, and longer cumulative exposure), people with diabetes (poor peripheral circulation and immune response create ideal conditions), people with weakened immune systems (including those on immunosuppressive medications), individuals with a history of athlete's foot, and people who regularly spend time in warm, moist communal environments such as gyms, pools, and locker rooms.

Nail growth rate is a surprisingly significant factor. Nails that grow slowly  a common feature of ageing and certain medical conditions  give fungi more time to establish before the infected portion grows out. Nails that are already structurally compromised (from psoriasis, injury, or repeated trauma) lack the seal that healthy nails provide. People who wear occlusive footwear for long periods, such as safety boots, create a warm, damp environment inside the shoe for hours each day.

There is also a genuine gender gap: <strong>nail fungus</strong> is more prevalent in men than women across most age groups, though women seek diagnosis and treatment at higher rates. This may relate to footwear choices, occupational exposure, or simply that the social visibility of feet differs between genders.

Family history plays a role too. Some people appear to have a genetic predisposition toward <strong>nail fungus</strong> infections, though the exact mechanism is not fully understood. If a close family member has struggled with repeated infections, that is relevant information when assessing your own risk.


<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Nail Fungus vs Conditions That Look Like It

Three conditions are commonly mistaken for <strong>nail fungus</strong>, and the confusion is understandable  they produce overlapping visual symptoms. Getting the distinction right matters because the treatments are entirely different, and applying antifungal treatment to a nail that has nail psoriasis, for example, will do precisely nothing.

The key differentiator for <strong>nail fungus</strong> versus nail psoriasis is the pattern of nail changes and their context. Nail psoriasis typically produces pitting (small depressions in the nail surface), onycholysis (nail lifting from the nail bed), and salmon-coloured patches under the nail  and crucially, it usually co-exists with psoriasis elsewhere on the body or a known psoriasis diagnosis. <strong>Nail fungus</strong> is more likely to begin at the nail tip and spread inward, producing yellow-brown discolouration and subungual debris. The two conditions can co-exist, which complicates diagnosis further  another reason that a GP's assessment rather than a pharmacist's guess is worth seeking for anything ambiguous.

Nail trauma is the second common lookalike. A nail that has experienced repeated microtrauma  from shoes, exercise, or occupational impact  can develop the same thickening, yellowing, and lifting that characterises <strong>nail fungus</strong>. The giveaway is history: trauma-related changes tend to be linked to a specific event or a specific nail (often the second toe in runners), while fungal infections tend to spread and involve adjacent nails over time.

The third is keratin granulations from nail polish  white chalky patches on the nail plate that appear after removing gel or regular polish and look alarmingly like the white superficial type of <strong>nail fungus</strong>. These are surface-level changes caused by dehydration of the nail plate and are not an infection at all. For more on what causes them and how to distinguish them, our article on <a href='/nail-faqs/can-nail-polish-damage-your-natural-nails'>keratin granulations from nail polish</a> covers this in detail.

If there is any genuine uncertainty about what you're looking at, confirmation matters. A GP can take a nail scraping and send it for culture  the only way to definitively confirm a fungal infection and identify the specific organism involved. The <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279547/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">clinical overview of nail fungus</a> at NCBI outlines the standard diagnostic approach.


