## Can Nail Fungus Spread  to Other Nails, People, or Surfaces?

Yes, <strong>nail fungus</strong> spreads  but it does so through specific, predictable routes rather than casual contact. Understanding those routes removes the anxiety and allows you to take targeted precautions rather than treating every surface as a hazard.

Spread to other nails is the most common form. When the infected nail contacts a healthy nail  during routine activities, while dressing, or when using the same nail clippers across all nails  the fungus can transfer. Clippers and files used on infected nails become vectors. This is the most frequent route through which a single infected nail becomes two or three over months.

Transmission between people does occur, but requires more sustained contact or shared items than most people realise. Walking through the same damp communal space does carry some risk, but direct transfer via shared towels, clippers, or files is a more significant route. <strong>Nail fungus</strong> is not airborne and is not passed by ordinary touch.

Surfaces retain fungal spores. Pool surrounds, communal shower floors, and gym changing rooms all carry a degree of risk, particularly in warmer, more humid environments. Wearing flip-flops in shared wet areas is a genuine protective measure, not just a social nicety.

The salon question deserves a direct answer. If you have <strong>nail fungus</strong> and book a pedicure, disclosing this to your technician is the right thing to do. Reputable salons have sterilisation protocols that can accommodate clients with fungal infections, and your technician can take appropriate precautions. Many technicians  as covered by the professional nail industry publication <a href="https://www.nailpro.com/onychomycosis-types-nail-fungus-causes-and-symptoms/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nailpro</a>  are trained to recognise and manage onychomycosis in a salon context.


