## When to Treat It Yourself and When to See a Doctor

Most people wait too long. The reasoning is understandable  <strong>nail fungus</strong> is not painful in its early stages, OTC products are readily available, and the idea of a GP appointment for a nail feels disproportionate. But waiting allows the infection to involve more of the nail and more nails, which directly reduces the effectiveness of any subsequent treatment.

<strong>Nail fungus</strong> almost never resolves without treatment. The nail plate is a physical barrier that protects the fungus inside it  no amount of scrubbing, air-drying, or home remedy penetrates the nail structure sufficiently to reach and clear the infection. Left alone, it progresses. The timeline varies, but without intervention the nail typically becomes increasingly thickened, more discoloured, and more separated from the nail bed over months to years.

OTC antifungal treatments  topical lacquers and solutions available without a prescription  are appropriate for mild infections: early-stage discolouration confined to less than half the nail, no involvement of the nail base (the lunula area), and ideally only one or two nails affected. Applied consistently over the required period, they can produce visible improvement in mild cases.

A GP or dermatologist visit is warranted when: the infection covers more than half the nail, multiple nails are involved, the nail base is affected (which suggests the nail matrix may be involved and topical treatment will be insufficient), there is pain, you have diabetes or any condition affecting circulation or immune response, or you've used OTC treatment for several months without improvement. Oral antifungal medications  prescription only in the UK  have significantly higher clearance rates than topical treatments but carry their own considerations, and a GP is the right person to assess those.

The framing that matters here: seeing a doctor about <strong>nail fungus</strong> is not an overreaction. It is appropriate medical use. Earlier treatment, matched to the correct type and severity of infection, produces better outcomes than months of self-managing something that isn't responding.


