## An Overview of Nail Fungus Treatment Options

Treatment works. That is worth saying first, because the community narrative around <strong>nail fungus</strong>  built on years of difficult, slow, and sometimes failed attempts  can make the whole thing sound hopeless. It isn't. But patience is genuinely part of the process, for a specific biological reason: nails grow slowly. Even when an antifungal clears the fungus itself, the evidence of that clearance only becomes visible as the healthy nail grows forward to replace the infected portion. For toenails, that can take six to eighteen months.

Topical antifungal treatments  lacquers and solutions applied directly to the nail  are the first approach for mild to moderate infections. Products containing amorolfine, ciclopirox, or efinaconazole are the most clinically supported options. The key to topical treatment success is nail preparation: keeping the nail filed back, removing as much of the thickened material as possible, and applying consistently to the clean nail surface. Sporadic application over months yields poor results.

Oral antifungal medications  terbinafine in particular  have significantly higher cure rates than topical treatments and are the standard of care for moderate to severe onychomycosis. They work systemically, reaching the nail matrix and nail bed from within. The treatment course is typically shorter than topical approaches (twelve weeks for fingernails, often sixteen to twenty-four weeks for toenails) but requires a prescription and appropriate monitoring, particularly around liver function.

Laser treatment exists and is available privately at some clinics, but the evidence base remains less robust than for systemic antifungals.

For a full comparison of OTC versus prescription options and what the evidence actually shows, see our guide to <a href='/blog/nail-fungus/nail-fungus-treatment'>nail fungus treatment</a>.


